insert into public.questions (id, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('q3-001',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$In an IaaS deployment, a virtual machine is breached through a missing OS patch. Under the shared responsibility model, who failed?$q$,$q$["The cloud provider - they own the hypervisor","The customer - guest OS patching is theirs in IaaS","Both equally - responsibility is always shared 50/50","The ISP - they carried the malicious traffic"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$In IaaS the provider secures hardware, facilities, and hypervisor; everything from the guest OS up - patching included - belongs to the customer. Responsibility splits by layer, not evenly; the ISP has no responsibility in this model.$q$),
('q3-002',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Which cloud model gives the customer the LEAST security responsibility?$q$,$q$["IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","On-premises private cloud"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$In SaaS the provider manages hardware through application; the customer handles mainly data, identities, and access configuration. IaaS leaves the most with the customer; on-premises leaves EVERYTHING with the customer.$q$),
('q3-003',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A company defines its entire cloud network - subnets, firewall rules, servers - in version-controlled templates. A reviewer notices a template exposes SSH to the world; the fix is one commit, redeployed everywhere. Which concept does this illustrate?$q$,$q$["Serverless computing","Infrastructure as Code","Software-defined WAN","Containerization"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Declaring infrastructure in reviewable, versioned code is IaC - misconfigurations become code review findings and fixes propagate consistently (the flip side: a bad template propagates the flaw everywhere too). Serverless is a compute model; SD-WAN is networking; containers package applications.$q$),
('q3-004',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the PRIMARY security advantage of serverless functions over customer-managed servers?$q$,$q$["Functions cannot be attacked","No operating system for the customer to patch or harden","Serverless code runs with unlimited privileges","Provider handles application logic flaws"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Serverless removes the guest OS from the customer's plate - the provider patches the platform. Functions absolutely can be attacked (event injection, IAM misconfig), and the CUSTOMER still owns code flaws and permission scoping.$q$),
('q3-005',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Compared to virtual machines, what is the key security limitation of containers?$q$,$q$["Containers cannot be scanned for vulnerabilities","All containers on a host share the host's kernel","Containers cannot use TLS","Container images cannot be signed"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Containers isolate at the OS level while SHARING the kernel - a kernel exploit or container escape threatens every container on the host, unlike hypervisor-isolated VMs. Scanning, TLS, and image signing all exist and are best practices.$q$),
('q3-006',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A water utility asks how to secure 20-year-old SCADA controllers that crash when port-scanned and cannot be patched. What is the BEST architecture approach?$q$,$q$["Schedule weekly vulnerability scans against them","Isolate them in a segmented OT network with strict boundary controls and passive monitoring","Move the controllers to the corporate VLAN for better visibility","Install endpoint agents on each controller"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Fragile, unpatchable ICS/OT gear is protected by isolation: dedicated segments, tightly controlled boundary (unidirectional where possible), and PASSIVE monitoring that never probes the devices. Active scanning crashes them; merging with IT networks increases exposure; legacy controllers cannot host agents.$q$),
('q3-007',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Which environment prioritizes AVAILABILITY and SAFETY over confidentiality?$q$,$q$["Corporate email","E-commerce web tier","Industrial control systems","HR records database"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$In ICS/OT, stopping a physical process can endanger lives and production - uptime and safety dominate; data secrecy is secondary. Email, e-commerce, and HR systems weight confidentiality/integrity much higher.$q$),
('q3-008',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the main security concern with IoT devices in an enterprise?$q$,$q$["They consume excessive bandwidth","Default credentials and unpatchable firmware create persistent weak points","They only support IPv6","They cannot join wireless networks"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$IoT ships with published default passwords, rarely receives updates, and lives for years - mitigate with inventory, credential changes, and network segmentation. Bandwidth, IPv6, and Wi-Fi capability are not the core risk.$q$),
('q3-009',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$High availability vs fault tolerance: a properly configured RAID 1 mirror that keeps serving through a disk failure demonstrates which?$q$,$q$["High availability with brief failover","Fault tolerance - the failure is invisible to users","Disaster recovery","Cold standby"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Fault tolerance means zero interruption - the mirrored disk absorbs the failure invisibly. HA generally allows brief failover blips; DR is recovery after major loss; cold standby requires manual startup.$q$),
('q3-010',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Where should an organization place public-facing web servers relative to its internal network?$q$,$q$["Directly on the internal LAN for performance","In a screened subnet between two firewall boundaries","Outside all firewalls","On the same VLAN as domain controllers"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Public services belong in a screened subnet (DMZ): internet can reach them under policy, but a compromise there still faces another firewall before the LAN. On-LAN or unfirewalled placement removes that containment; colocating with domain controllers is the worst option.$q$),
('q3-011',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A security-critical firewall loses power. Traffic stops flowing entirely until it recovers. Which design principle does this reflect?$q$,$q$["Fail-open","Fail-closed","Load balancing","Active-active clustering"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Blocking everything on failure is fail-closed - security preserved at availability's expense, correct for a perimeter firewall. Fail-open would pass traffic uninspected; clustering/load balancing are availability designs, not failure modes.$q$),
('q3-012',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$What is the functional difference between an IDS and an IPS?$q$,$q$["IDS is hardware; IPS is software","IDS detects and alerts from a traffic copy; IPS sits inline and can block","IDS works on wireless only; IPS on wired","IDS encrypts traffic; IPS decrypts it"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An IDS passively watches mirrored/tapped traffic and alerts (detective); an IPS is inline in the traffic path and can drop malicious packets (preventive) - at the cost of latency and a potential failure point. Form factor, media, and encryption are irrelevant distinctions.$q$),
('q3-013',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Administrators must reach segmented production servers only through a single hardened, heavily logged intermediary host. What is this host called?$q$,$q$["Reverse proxy","Jump server","Load balancer","NAC appliance"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A jump server (bastion) is the controlled, audited gateway for administrative access into sensitive zones. Reverse proxies front APPLICATION traffic for servers; load balancers distribute traffic; NAC gates network admission by posture.$q$),
('q3-014',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which device receives inbound client requests on behalf of web servers, terminates TLS, and can host a WAF?$q$,$q$["Forward proxy","Reverse proxy","Jump server","IDS sensor"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A reverse proxy fronts SERVERS: TLS termination, load distribution, caching, WAF integration. A forward proxy fronts CLIENTS heading out (filtering/anonymity); jump servers broker admin access; IDS sensors only observe.$q$),
('q3-015',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$An IPS must be deployed so its hardware failure cannot take down the network link. Which requirement addresses this?$q$,$q$["Deploy in fail-closed mode","Use a fail-open bypass (or passive tap) so traffic flows if the device dies","Configure signature updates","Place it behind the firewall"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$For an inline device where AVAILABILITY of the link outranks inspection, fail-open (hardware bypass) keeps traffic flowing on device failure. Fail-closed would cut the link - correct for some security postures but contrary to the stated requirement. Updates and placement do not address device failure.$q$),
('q3-016',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$What does 802.1X provide at the switch port or wireless AP?$q$,$q$["Encryption of all LAN traffic","Authentication before network access is granted","Automatic VLAN trunking","MAC address randomization"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$802.1X is port-based network access control: the supplicant must authenticate (via the authenticator to a RADIUS server) before the port passes traffic. It authenticates access; it is not a bulk encryption, trunking, or MAC privacy mechanism.$q$),
('q3-017',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A site-to-site VPN must encrypt entire IP packets, including original headers, between two office gateways. Which configuration is correct?$q$,$q$["IPSec AH in transport mode","IPSec ESP in tunnel mode","TLS in transport mode","GRE without encryption"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$ESP provides encryption; tunnel mode encapsulates the ENTIRE original packet (headers included) - the standard gateway-to-gateway VPN. AH authenticates but does not encrypt; transport mode protects only the payload (host-to-host); GRE alone has no confidentiality.$q$),
('q3-018',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Remote workers' internet traffic goes directly out their home connection while only corporate-bound traffic uses the VPN. What is this configuration and its trade-off?$q$,$q$["Full tunnel - all traffic inspected, higher latency","Split tunnel - better performance, less corporate visibility/control","Clientless VPN - browser only","Always-on VPN - cannot be disabled"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Splitting corporate traffic (tunneled) from general internet (direct) is split tunneling: faster and cheaper, but corporate security stack never sees the direct traffic. Full tunnel inspects everything at latency/bandwidth cost.$q$),
('q3-019',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which technology delivers SD-WAN plus cloud-based security services (ZTNA, SWG, CASB, FWaaS) as a unified offering for a distributed workforce?$q$,$q$["MPLS","SASE","NAC","DNSSEC"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Secure Access Service Edge converges networking and security in the cloud, applying consistent policy wherever users are. MPLS is a WAN transport; NAC controls LAN admission; DNSSEC signs DNS records.$q$),
('q3-020',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$Customer records stored on an encrypted database volume are in which data state?$q$,$q$["In transit","In use","At rest","In processing"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Stored data is at rest - protected here by encryption at rest. In transit means moving across networks (TLS territory); in use/processing means loaded in memory being computed on.$q$),
('q3-021',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Why is data IN USE the hardest state to protect?$q$,$q$["It moves too fast to encrypt","It must be decrypted in memory for processing","Users refuse to protect it","It is always on removable media"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$CPUs compute on plaintext, so data in use sits decrypted in RAM - exposed to memory scraping and privileged malware. Protections shift to access control, isolation, and secure enclaves/confidential computing.$q$),
('q3-022',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A French regulator requires that citizens' data collected in France remain stored on servers physically located in the EU. Which concept is at play?$q$,$q$["Data masking","Data sovereignty","Data retention","Non-repudiation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Data being subject to the laws of where it physically resides - and requirements to keep it in-jurisdiction - is data sovereignty, driving geographic storage restrictions. Masking obscures display; retention governs how long data is kept.$q$),
('q3-023',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A support screen shows customers' card numbers as ****-****-****-4821. Which protection method is this?$q$,$q$["Tokenization","Encryption","Masking","Hashing"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Obscuring characters for DISPLAY while the full value exists underneath is masking - it limits what shoulder-surfers and low-privilege staff see. Tokenization substitutes stored values; encryption/hashing transform the stored data itself.$q$),
('q3-024',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Which classification pairing is CORRECT for a typical commercial scheme?$q$,$q$["Public: employee salaries","Confidential: marketing brochures","Restricted: trade-secret formula","Internal: press releases"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$The crown-jewel trade secret belongs at the highest tier (restricted/critical). Salaries are confidential (not public); brochures and press releases are public (not confidential/internal). Protections must scale with the tier.$q$),
('q3-025',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A financial firm needs a recovery site that can take over within minutes with near-current data. Which site type and why?$q$,$q$["Cold site - cheapest","Warm site - balance of cost and speed","Hot site - fully equipped with replicated data","Mobile site - deployable anywhere"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Minutes-level RTO with current data demands a hot site: running infrastructure plus continuous replication - the most expensive option, justified by the recovery requirement. Warm sites have stale data (hours-days); cold sites are empty space (days-weeks).$q$),
('q3-026',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Two data centers are placed 500 miles apart. Which resilience consideration does this address?$q$,$q$["Platform diversity","Geographic dispersion","Capacity planning","Vendor diversity"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Distance ensures one regional disaster (hurricane, quake, grid collapse) cannot destroy both sites - geographic dispersion. Platform/vendor diversity avoids common technology failure; capacity planning sizes resources.$q$),
('q3-027',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Which RAID level survives TWO simultaneous disk failures?$q$,$q$["RAID 0","RAID 1","RAID 5","RAID 6"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$RAID 6 uses dual parity - any two disks can fail. RAID 5 (single parity) and RAID 1 (mirror) survive one; RAID 0 (striping) survives none - one failure loses everything.$q$),
('q3-028',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$Backups run: full on Sunday, incrementals Monday-Saturday. The server dies Thursday morning. Which backups are needed to restore, in order?$q$,$q$["Sunday full only","Sunday full + Wednesday incremental only","Sunday full + Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday incrementals","Wednesday incremental only"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Incrementals capture changes since the LAST backup of any type, so restore requires the full plus EVERY incremental since: Sunday + Mon + Tue + Wed. (With DIFFERENTIALS - changes since last full - it would be Sunday + Wednesday only.)$q$),
('q3-029',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$What advantage do DIFFERENTIAL backups have over INCREMENTAL backups?$q$,$q$["Smaller daily backup size","Faster restores - only the full plus the latest differential are needed","They never need a full backup","They deduplicate automatically"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Differentials accumulate all changes since the last FULL, so restore = full + newest differential (two sets). The cost: each differential grows through the week, while incrementals stay small but restore chains grow long.$q$),
('q3-030',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Why must at least one backup copy be offline or immutable?$q$,$q$["Regulations require tape","Ransomware actively seeks and encrypts reachable online backups","Online storage is too slow","Immutable copies compress better"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Modern ransomware deletes or encrypts any backup it can reach over the network before detonating. Offline (air-gapped) or immutable (write-once) copies survive - the heart of the 3-2-1 rule. The other options are not the reason.$q$),
('q3-031',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$During a power outage, which component keeps servers running for the 45 seconds the generator needs to start?$q$,$q$["RAID controller","UPS","Managed PDU","Redundant NIC"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The UPS battery bridges the gap instantly until generator power stabilizes (or triggers graceful shutdown for short-only coverage). RAID protects disks; PDUs distribute power; NICs handle networking.$q$),
('q3-032',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Which continuity test provides the MOST realistic validation with the MOST operational risk?$q$,$q$["Tabletop exercise","Simulation","Full failover test","Plan review"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Actually switching production to backup systems (failover test) proves recovery genuinely works - and risks real disruption if it does not. Tabletops and simulations build confidence cheaply but validate less.$q$),
('q3-033',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A switch port is configured to accept only one specific MAC address and shut down if another appears. What is this feature?$q$,$q$["802.1X","Port security","NAC posture check","VLAN tagging"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Limiting learned MAC addresses per port (with violation actions) is switch port security - a basic guard against rogue devices and MAC flooding. 802.1X authenticates users/devices cryptographically; NAC adds posture; VLAN tagging segments traffic.$q$),
('q3-034',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which firewall generation can identify the specific APPLICATION (not just port) generating traffic and enforce per-user policies?$q$,$q$["Packet-filtering firewall","Stateful L4 firewall","Next-generation firewall","Personal host firewall"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$NGFWs are application-aware (deep packet inspection, user identity, integrated IPS) - they see WhatsApp inside 443, not just port 443. Packet filters and stateful L4 firewalls act on addresses/ports/state only.$q$),
('q3-035',3,'3.2','matching',2,$q$Match each insecure protocol to its secure replacement.$q$,$q${"left":["Telnet","HTTP","FTP","SNMPv2"],"right":["SSH","HTTPS","SFTP","SNMPv3"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$SSH replaces Telnet (encrypted terminal, port 22). HTTPS wraps HTTP in TLS (443). SFTP runs file transfer over SSH (FTPS is the TLS alternative). SNMPv3 adds authentication and encryption absent in v1/v2c.$q$),
('q3-036',3,'3.4','ordering',2,$q$Order the recovery site types from FASTEST to SLOWEST recovery.$q$,$q$["Hot site","Warm site","Cold site"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Hot: running systems + replicated data = minutes to hours. Warm: equipment present, data restored on activation = hours to days. Cold: space, power, and cooling only = days to weeks. Cost ranks in the same order.$q$),
('q3-037',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$An architect must choose between synchronous and asynchronous replication to a DR site 800 miles away. Which statement decides it?$q$,$q$["Synchronous is always better","At that distance, synchronous latency is prohibitive - asynchronous with a small RPO gap is the practical choice","Asynchronous guarantees zero data loss","Replication type does not affect RPO"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Synchronous replication makes every write wait for the remote site - physics makes that painful beyond metro distances. Async decouples writes, accepting seconds-to-minutes of potential loss (RPO > 0). Zero-data-loss claims belong to synchronous; replication choice directly SETS the achievable RPO.$q$),
('q3-038',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the security significance of VM sprawl?$q$,$q$["VMs consume too much RAM","Unmanaged, forgotten VMs miss patches and monitoring, becoming easy footholds","Hypervisors limit VM count","Sprawl breaks live migration"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Sprawl means VMs created outside governance: absent from inventory, unpatched, unmonitored - exactly the systems attackers find first. The concern is security debt, not resource usage or hypervisor limits.$q$),
('q3-039',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Which architecture consideration asks: how quickly can we bring this system back after failure?$q$,$q$["Ease of deployment","Ease of recovery","Patch availability","Inability to patch"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Ease of recovery is the exam's term for restore speed/simplicity after failure - influenced by automation, IaC redeployability, and backup design. Deployment ease covers initial rollout; the patch considerations cover maintainability.$q$),
('q3-040',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A company enables TLS inspection on its egress proxy. Which consequence must the security team plan for?$q$,$q$["Traffic becomes unencrypted on the internet","Client devices must trust the proxy's CA certificate, and some apps with pinned certificates will break","DNS stops resolving","TLS 1.3 becomes mandatory everywhere"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$TLS inspection re-signs sessions with the proxy's CA, so endpoints must trust that CA - and certificate-pinning apps (banking, some updaters) will refuse the substituted cert and need bypass rules. External traffic stays encrypted; DNS and TLS versions are unaffected.$q$),
('q3-041',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Which pairing of data type to example is CORRECT?$q$,$q$["Regulated data: company org chart","Trade secret: unreleased product formula","Intellectual property: cafeteria menu","Financial information: public stock price"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A secret formula is the definitional trade secret - value comes from secrecy. Org charts are internal (not regulated); menus are public (not IP); already-public stock prices need no protection (internal financials do).$q$),
('q3-042',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$What is capacity planning's PEOPLE dimension?$q$,$q$["Buying more servers","Ensuring enough trained staff exist to operate and recover systems, including coverage for departures","Hiring only contractors","Reducing headcount through automation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The exam's capacity planning spans people, technology, and infrastructure - the people part means sufficient trained personnel (and cross-training to survive departures). One resignation should not eliminate the only person who can run recovery.$q$),
('q3-043',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A startup runs entirely on one cloud provider. The board asks about resilience if that provider has a major regional outage. Which TWO-part strategy addresses this?$q$,$q$["Buy more instances in the same zone","Multi-region deployment within the provider, plus evaluating multi-cloud for critical workloads","Move everything on-premises immediately","Rely on the provider's SLA refund"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Multi-region survives regional failures; platform/vendor diversity (multi-cloud) hedges provider-wide failure for the most critical services. Same-zone scaling shares the failure domain; SLA credits refund pennies, not lost business.$q$),
('q3-044',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which statement about agent-based vs agentless (clientless) VPN access is correct?$q$,$q$["Agent-based requires installed software and offers full network access; clientless works through a browser for specific apps","Clientless offers more access than agent-based","Agent-based cannot enforce posture checks","Clientless encrypts nothing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The installed VPN client (agent) tunnels at the network layer - broad access plus posture checking; clientless/browser VPN portals expose selected applications only - convenient for contractors/BYOD. The remaining statements invert or falsify these properties.$q$),
('q3-045',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Why must backups be TESTED with actual restores, not just verified as completing?$q$,$q$["Restore tests are required for tax purposes","Completed jobs can still yield unrestorable data - corruption, missing dependencies, lost keys surface only on restore","Testing makes backups run faster","Untested backups expire"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A green checkmark proves a job ran, not that data comes BACK: silent corruption, unbacked-up dependencies, and inaccessible encryption keys are discovered only by restoring. Recovery testing (including timed drills against RTO) is the only proof.$q$);
